{"qid":"Q19336869","names":["County of Blôis"],"title":"County of Blôis","endonym":null,"capsule":"The County of Blois was a major feudal principality of the Loire valley whose counts, beginning with Thibault I ('the Trickster') in the 10th century, rivaled the Capetian kings of France by controlling a constellation of territories — Blois, Chartres, Champagne, and Meaux — that encircled the royal domain. Thibault III and his successors made the county one of the most powerful fiefs of 11th-century France. The line passed through the Châtillon family and by the 14th century Blois was separated from Champagne; Louis I, Duke of Orléans purchased the county in 1397 from the last Châtillon count, Guy II, and it subsequently passed into the Orléans appanage. The county was absorbed into the French royal domain in 1498 when Louis XII — himself Count of Blois before his accession — became king.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The comital succession is well attested in French royal and ecclesiastical records, though the county's territorial boundaries fluctuated considerably and cartographic representations are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Blois","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Blois-France"},{"label":"Wikipedia: County of Blois","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_Blois"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"County of Blois","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Odo I","nativeName":"Eudes Ier","roman":"Eudes I","start":977,"end":996,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Theobald II","nativeName":"Thibault II","roman":"Thibault